<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    ServiceNow Update Set record-definition for the foundational `x_casemgmt_case`
    custom table inside the scoped application `x_casemgmt`.

    This table is the case-lifecycle anchor of the POC. It replaces the case-domain
    subset of ArkCase's `acm_case_file` MySQL table (the JPA entity `CaseFile`
    in acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-case-file-plugin/src/main/java/com/
    armedia/acm/plugins/casefile/model/CaseFile.java) - converting the JPA
    @Entity / @Table(name="acm_case_file") / @Inheritance(SINGLE_TABLE) pattern
    into a ServiceNow scoped table whose discriminator collapses into a `type`
    Choice rather than a class-name discriminator column.

    This record authors ONLY table-level metadata (name, labels, parent class,
    scope linkage, auto-numbering reference, and access flags). The 14 fields
    on this table (number, type, status, priority, subject, description,
    opened_date, closed_date, assigned_group, assigned_agent, requester_name,
    requester_email — the 12 AAP-enumerated user-prompt fields — plus the
    internal pending_reason choice and the virtual duration_to_close Function
    Field) are authored as sys_dictionary records in the sibling
    ../dictionary/ folder. The platform automatically inherits the standard
    sys_* audit columns (sys_id,
    sys_created_on, sys_created_by, sys_updated_on, sys_updated_by) by virtue
    of being a sys_db_object record.

    Constraints honored:
      - Scoped-namespace exclusivity: every cross-reference resolves into the
        x_casemgmt scope; no global-scope writes.
      - Zero hardcoded sys_id literals in any reference field; sys_scope,
        sys_package, and number_ref all resolve by scope name via the `source`
        attribute. The sys_id element below is THIS record's own primary key
        (deterministic MD5 of the table name) and is not a reference to any
        other record.
      - super_class is empty: this table extends NOTHING. It does not extend
        the OOB `task` table (which would write to global scope and violate
        AAP Section 0.3.2). The platform still provides standard sys_* audit columns
        because sys_db_object records always do.
      - create_access_controls is false: ServiceNow auto-creates a default ACL
        set when this is true. Since ../acl/ enumerates a precise role x table
        x CRUD matrix with custom "Assigned only" conditions for case_agent,
        the auto-generated ACLs would conflict and are explicitly suppressed
        here.
      - CROSS-SCOPE ACCESS IS LEAST PRIVILEGE: READ ONLY, AND THE COLUMNS ARE
        BOOLEAN, NOT STRINGS. On sys_db_object only `access` is a string
        (public | package_private); ws_access, read_access, create_access,
        update_access, delete_access, alter_access, client_scripts_access and
        configuration_access are all `boolean` (verified against sys_dictionary
        on the target release). Assigning the string "public" to a boolean column
        stores FALSE - an earlier revision of this artifact did exactly that -
        and the platform then refuses EVERY cross-scope operation on this table:
        "Read operation against 'x_casemgmt_case' from scope 'rhino.global' has
        been refused due to the table's cross-scope access policy".
        Exactly two columns are opened below, each because a capability
        measurably depends on the READ it permits:
          1. read_access : the REST Table API post-commit verification gate
             (GET /api/now/table/x_casemgmt_case answered HTTP 403 as admin);
             any Global-scope verification script (a Global GlideRecord read
             silently reported getRowCount() == 0 on a fully populated table);
             and the ATF client test runner, because the platform's own
             Global-scope Script Include TestExecutorAjax.validateFormParameters
             resolves the record for the "Open an Existing Record" step with a
             plain GlideRecord, so without it every form-level test failed with
             "Table 'x_casemgmt_case' does not have a record with id '...'".
          2. ws_access : the same REST Table API path, which is refused outright
             when this column is false.
        create_access, update_access and delete_access are deliberately FALSE.
        Application Access is a SEPARATE gate from the record ACLs: a Global or
        foreign-scoped script using plain GlideRecord is not filtered by
        ../acl/*.xml at all (only GlideRecordSecure applies record ACLs), and
        Global code can additionally suppress this application's before-update
        transition guards with setWorkflow(false). Leaving the three write
        columns open therefore allowed any Global caller to create, modify or
        delete case rows outside the manager/agent/viewer matrix of AAP
        Section 0.5.6 and outside the state machine of AAP Section 0.5.5 -
        measured on the target PDI, where a Global-scope GlideRecord insert,
        update and delete against this table all succeeded. Closing them costs
        this deliverable nothing: the seed script runs in scope, the portal REST
        endpoints and every Script Include are in scope, the remediation script
        writes only platform tables, and the ATF suite scored 16 of 20 with all
        five columns false - the four failures being the Global READ path above
        (ATF 15/16/17) and an unrelated ACL compile defect (ATF 07), with zero
        failures among the 26 Record Update, 6 Record Insert and 7 Record Delete
        steps that write these tables.
        alter_access, client_scripts_access and configuration_access stay FALSE:
        no other scope may reshape this table's schema, client scripts or
        configuration. All eight are stated explicitly below rather than left to
        a mis-typed string coercing to false.
      - CACHE NOTE for anyone repairing a live instance by hand: writing these
        columns flushes only the sys_db_object catalog, NOT
        syscache_tabledescriptor, so the new values appear in the record while
        the old policy is still enforced. Touch this table's collection
        sys_dictionary row (name=<table>, element=NULL) afterwards to force the
        descriptor rebuild. ../scripts/post_import_remediation.js does exactly
        that and then verifies the effective access.
      - The concrete scope identifier casemgmt is used throughout per AAP convention; ServiceNow Update Set imports use a standard XML parser, so the scope id is concrete in the XML before export.
-->
<record_update table="sys_db_object">
